user
We are doing a Cisco-only 4-hour real-lab automation run.

Work only in:
/home/administrator/infra-config-portal

Use:
PROVIDER_MODE=local-lab-readwrite
.env.local.real-lab

Goal:
Figure out the Cisco switch console automatically, configure the switch for lab management, and validate Ethernet/SSH/SCP readiness.

Context:
- Cisco switch is connected by USB serial adapter.
- The app must discover the adapter by itself.
- The switch also has Ethernet connected for management after bootstrap.
- Cisco management is not configured yet.
- This run is allowed to configure the lab switch.
- Reboot/reload is allowed only if the workflow needs it and records why.

Main run stages:

Stage 1: Baseline
- Check git status.
- Read existing Cisco readiness code and reports.
- Save a starting report to artifacts/codex-runs/cisco-4h-lab-run-report.md.

Stage 2: Console adapter discovery
- Enumerate /dev/serial/by-id, /dev/ttyUSB*, /dev/ttyACM*.
- Pick the most stable console path.
- Detect whether another process owns the port.
- Save adapter details.

Stage 3: Console prompt detection
- Try common baud rates:
  9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, 115200.
- Try newline, Enter, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z as needed.
- Detect prompts:
  >, #, Switch>, Switch#, Router>, Router#, Username:, Password:, initial config dialog.
- Capture redacted console samples to artifacts.
- If login is required, use configured credentials from .env.local.real-lab without printing secrets.

Stage 4: Switch identification and current-state capture
- Identify model, version, hostname, interfaces, VLANs, management interface, SSH status, SCP status.
- Capture current running config/status into redacted artifacts.
- Do not use mock results.

Stage 5: Build Cisco bootstrap plan
Use lab config from .env.local.real-lab where available.
If config names differ, inspect existing config.py/env docs to determine current variable names.

The bootstrap plan should cover:
- hostname
- management VLAN
- management IP
- default gateway
- local admin user
- SSH version 2
- SCP enablement
- DNS/domain if configured
- LLDP
- disable HTTP/HTTPS server if that is part of existing app policy
- line console/vty login local
- per-port defaults if already represented in the app

Stage 6: Apply Cisco bootstrap over console
- Apply the planned config through console.
- Save the commands sent in redacted form.
- Handle initial setup dialog if present.
- Save report.

Stage 7: Save and reload if needed
- Write memory / copy running-config startup-config.
- Reload/reboot only if required for the workflow or if the switch needs it after bootstrap.
- If reloaded, wait for console to return and recapture identity/status.

Stage 8: Ethernet management validation
- Test ping/SSH to the configured management IP.
- Validate SSH readiness.
- Validate SCP readiness if supported.
- Save Ethernet readiness report.

Stage 9: UI and tests
- Update Cisco page/operator UI so it shows:
  console adapter detected,
  prompt detected,
  switch identity,
  bootstrap plan,
  apply status,
  save/reload status,
  Ethernet management status,
  SSH/SCP status,
  last blocker.
- Run relevant backend tests.
- Run frontend build.
- Save final report:
  artifacts/codex-runs/cisco-4h-lab-run-report.md.

Keep going until the 4-hour timeout or a real physical blocker.
When blocked, improve the app/reporting so the blocker is obvious.
Do not use mock results as substitutes for real lab results.
Do not print secrets.
